course: dictionary
lesson: 13274
title: Gorgeous and its many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Gorgeous means very beautiful, or very pleasant and enjoyable. Writers choose it when they want a stronger word than pretty or nice. «The sunset over the sea was gorgeous.» Here it means the sunset was very beautiful.
explain: The word also describes things you enjoy a lot, like weather, food or a day out. «We had gorgeous weather for the picnic, and the cake was gorgeous too.» Try swapping in nice: the sentence still works, but gorgeous tells the reader how much you liked it.
explain: In the UK, people sometimes say «Hello, gorgeous!» to a friend. This is informal and friendly, not a comment on how someone looks. Use the context to decide: a view is being described in one sentence, and a greeting is being given in the other.
fact: Gorgeous means very beautiful, or very pleasant and enjoyable.
fact: «Gorgeous» is a stronger word than «pretty» or «nice».
fact: «A gorgeous view» means a very beautiful view.
fact: Gorgeous can describe food, weather, clothes, babies and pets, as well as places and people.
fact: In informal UK English, «gorgeous» can be a friendly way to address someone, as in «Hello, gorgeous!»
exercise: «The view from the top of the hill was gorgeous.» What does gorgeous mean here? | *Very beautiful | Very cold | Very tiny | Very noisy
exercise: Mum says, «The soup is gorgeous!» What does she mean? | It has been left out all day | It is too hot to eat | It has no salt in it | *It tastes very good
exercise: «The puppy was gorgeous, with soft ears and a wagging tail.» Which word is closest in meaning to gorgeous here? | angry | sleepy | heavy | *lovely
test: «We ate a gorgeous cake.» Why is gorgeous a better word than nice here? | It means the cake was made by a machine | *It shows the writer enjoyed the cake very much | It means the cake was stale | It means the cake was small
test: A friend says, «Hello, gorgeous!» What does this show? | It describes a beautiful painting | It is a rude command | *It is a friendly, informal greeting | It is a question about the time
exam: Which sentence uses gorgeous to mean very pleasant? | *We spent a gorgeous afternoon by the lake, warm and calm | The sock was gorgeous and very small | The pencil was gorgeous and had no point | The road was gorgeous, full of potholes
